<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE concept PUBLIC "-//OASIS//DTD DITA Concept//EN" "concept.dtd">
<concept id="structured_authoring_resources">
    <title>Structured authoring resources</title>
    <shortdesc>Structured authoring is an important concept in technical writing you should
        understand.</shortdesc>
    <conbody>
        <p>I recommend you review these articles to get a better grasp on structured authoring.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><xref href="https://www.scriptorium.com/structure.pdf" format="pdf" scope="external"
                    >Structured Authoring and XML</xref></li>
            <li><xref href="https://www.madcapsoftware.com/blog/what-is-structured-authoring/"
                    format="html" scope="external"/></li>
            <li><xref href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Structured_writing" format="html"
                    scope="external"/></li>
        </ul>
        <p>The following isn't technically about structured authoring, but is a real world example
            of how structured data can improve search results. </p>
        <p><xref
                href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/intro-structured-data?visit_id=638791514022091018-1227983788&amp;hl=en&amp;rd=1"
                format="html" scope="external"/></p>
        <p>Today, understanding how structured content can help you prepare your content for AI is
            also important.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><xref
                    href="https://paligo.net/blog/how-structured-authoring-delivers-ai-ready-content-in-the-age-of-generative-ai/"
                    format="html" scope="external"/></li>
            <li><xref
                    href="https://thinkingdocumentation.com/blog/f/the-wild-west-of-ai-content-management-and-metadata"
                    format="html" scope="external"/> (This may be a scary deep dive, and requires
                further reading.)</li>
        </ul>
    </conbody>
</concept>
